<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Imola</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Imola is a town in Emilia-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romagna">Romagna</a>, northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/via-aemilia">Via Emilia</a> between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bologna">Bologna</a> and Faenza. Ruled in the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> by local lords and at times by the Manfredi of Faenza, it was given by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-sixtus-iv">Pope Sixtus IV</a> to his nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/girolamo-riario">Girolamo Riario</a> in 1473. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cesare-borgia">Cesare Borgia</a> took it from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caterina-sforza">Caterina Sforza</a> in 1499, it returned to direct papal rule.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/imola.jpg" alt="Imola" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Marpol · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Autodromo_aerea_poster.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Imola is a city and comune in the Metropolitan City of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bologna">Bologna</a>, on the river Santerno in the Emilia-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romagna">Romagna</a> region. It is traditionally considered the western entrance to the historical region of Romagna. Its Roman name was Forum Cornelii, the Forum of Cornelius, after the dictator <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulla">Sulla</a>, who founded the city around 82 BCE. It was an agricultural and trading centre, notable for its ceramics. The name Imola was first used in the 7th century by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombards</a>, who applied it to the fortress now known as the Castellaccio.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paul-the-deacon">Paul the Deacon</a>, Imola was in 412 the scene of the marriage of Ataulf, king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visigoths">Visigoths</a>, to Galla Placidia, daughter of the emperor Theodosius the Great. With the exarchate of Ravenna it passed under papal authority. In the 10th century and after, it saw incessant wars against Ravenna, Faenza and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bologna">Bologna</a>, and out of these conflicts came the city's republican constitution. In the contest between pope and emperor it was generally Ghibelline. Pope Benedict XII made Lippo II Alidosi pontifical vicar, and his family held power until 1424, when the condottiero Angelo della Pergola gained the upper hand.</p><p>In 1426 the city was restored to the Holy See, and it was conferred on the Sforza, dukes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>, in 1434, 1438 and 1470. It was later bestowed as a dowry on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caterina-sforza">Caterina Sforza</a>, bride of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/girolamo-riario">Girolamo Riario</a>, nephew of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-sixtus-iv">Pope Sixtus IV</a>, and under the Riarii it was embellished with palaces and works of art. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-alexander-vi">Pope Alexander VI</a> deprived Girolamo's son Ottaviano of power, and on 25 November 1499 the city surrendered to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cesare-borgia">Cesare Borgia</a>. In 1504 Imola submitted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-julius-ii">Pope Julius II</a>. It remained papal, apart from the years after 1797, until it joined the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> in 1860.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Imola is best known for the Autodromo Enzo e Dino Ferrari, opened in the 1950s. Among its older buildings are the Rocca Sforzesca, built under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/girolamo-riario">Girolamo Riario</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caterina-sforza">Caterina Sforza</a>, the cathedral dedicated to San Cassiano, erected between 1187 and 1271, and Palazzo Tozzoni, built between 1726 and 1738 and since 1981 a civic art museum.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Imola” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Between 1499 and 1501 Cesare Borgia, son of Pope Alexander VI, conquered the small lordships of Romagna and the Marche, including <b>Imola</b>, Forlì, Pesaro, Rimini and Faenza, with French and papal troops.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-romagna">Duchy of Romagna</a></li><li><blockquote>The Manfredi governed Faenza and neighbouring towns such as <b>Imola</b> for a time, often serving as condottieri for Florence, Milan or Venice.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-faenza">Lordship of Faenza</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1473 Pope Sixtus IV acquired <b>Imola</b> for his nephew Girolamo Riario, who married Caterina Sforza, daughter of Galeazzo Maria Sforza of the Duchy of Milan.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-imola-and-forli">Lordship of Imola and Forli</a></li></ul></section>
